<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry II the Pious</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1196–<a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry II of Silesia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry II the Pious was Duke of Silesia and high duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1238, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry the Bearded</a> and Saint Hedwig. Like his father he sought to reunite the divided Polish duchies. He was killed fighting the Mongol invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnica">Battle of Legnica</a> in April 1241, after which Silesia was divided among his sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious.jpg" alt="Henry II the Pious" width="303" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henryk_II_Pobo%C5%BCny_(Hedwig_Codex).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was the second son of High Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry the Bearded</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Hedwig of Silesia</a>. His elder brother Bolesław died in 1206, and in 1213 his younger brother Konrad the Curly died during a hunt, leaving Henry as sole heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>. His father arranged his marriage to Anne, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-i-of-bohemia">Ottokar I of Bohemia</a>. From 1222 he countersigned his father's documents, and when Henry the Bearded was captured in 1229 he acted as interim duke. When his father died on 19 March 1238 he became duke of Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and Greater Poland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry II was regent of the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a> and of Sandomierz while their rulers were minors, but he resigned the regency in 1239. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic">Władysław Odonic</a> on 5 June 1239 let him take most of his lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>. He abandoned his family's alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a>, which settled his dispute with the Church. He ended the conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad of Masovia</a> by marrying two of his daughters to two of Konrad's sons, and in 1239 he won the Battle of Lubusz and recovered the fortress of Santok.</p><p>In February 1241 a Mongol army of about 10,000 men under Orda invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and destroyed much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and Sandomierz nobility at Tursko and Chmielnik. Henry gathered troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>. Many European rulers, busy with the quarrel between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, did not answer his appeals; only a contingent sent by King Wenceslaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> joined him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry was defeated and killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnica">Battle of Legnica</a> on 9 April 1241. His wife identified his decapitated body by the six toes on his left foot, a detail confirmed when his tomb was opened in 1832. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> did not try to occupy Silesia and moved on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. His death split the Silesian Piast line into many ducal branches, and later traditions portrayed him as an ideal Christian knight and martyr. In October 2015 the Roman Catholic Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> opened his cause for beatification, giving him the title Servant of God.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry II the Pious” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Mongol force under Baidar and Kadan crushed the army of Duke <b>Henry II the Pious</b>, who was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Konrad I, a son of <b>Henry II the Pious</b>, received Głogów and the lands around it in a division of the Silesian inheritance around 1251.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glogow">Duchy of Głogów</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1248 partition of the Duchy of Silesia, Bolesław II the Bald, son of <b>Henry II the Pious</b> who died fighting the Mongols near Legnica in 1241, received the duchy around Legnica.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-legnica">Duchy of Legnica</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry I and <b>Henry II the Pious</b> tried to reunite Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Duchy of Silesia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1248 the sons of <b>Henry II the Pious</b> divided the Duchy of Silesia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-wroclaw">Duchy of Wrocław</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Legnickie Pole, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1225" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry II the Pious"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4450 5804h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4450 5804h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1225">What was here in 1225? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
